13:30:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fantastic observation that gets to the heart of how different types of flight work. You've correctly noticed that a fighter jet in a vertical climb seems to defy the "wings create lift" rule. The short answer is: **In a vertical climb, the wings are *not* lifting the plane. The engine's thrust is doing all the work to fight gravity.** Let's break it down. ### The Key Distinction: Lift vs. Thrust - **Lift** is the force that acts **perpendicular** to the direction of flight (straight up relative to the wings). - **Thrust** is the force that acts **parallel** to the direction of flight (straight forward out the back of the engine). In normal, level flight, the plane's path is horizontal. The wings generate lift straight up, and the engine generates thrust straight forward. These are two separate, perpendicular forces. ### The Vertical Climb: Thrust Becomes the "Lift" When a fighter jet climbs straight up: 1. **The plane's path is vertical.** So "forward" is now "upward." 2. **Thrust is now pointing straight up** (because the engine pushes the plane in the direction it's pointing). 3. **The wings are now "sideways" relative to the direction of motion.** They are slicing through the air like a knife, generating almost no lift at all. In fact, they create a lot of **drag** (air resistance) trying to slow the plane down. 4. **Gravity is pulling the plane straight down.** To keep climbing upward, the engine's thrust must be **stronger than the plane's weight**. So, a fighter jet can climb vertically because its engine produces **more thrust than the plane weighs**. This is called having a **thrust-to-weight ratio greater than 1**. For example, the F-15 can produce about 1.2 pounds of thrust for every pound it weighs. That extra 0.2 pounds of thrust is what accelerates it upward. ### Why Do Fighter Jets Still Have Wings at All? If the engine can just push the plane straight up, why not just make a rocket? Because staying in the air for long periods and turning efficiently requires **lift**. Wings are still essential for: - **Efficient cruise:** It takes far less energy to fly level on wings than to burn massive amounts of fuel pointing the engine straight up. - **Turning:** To turn, you need to bank (tilt) the wings. This points part of the lift force sideways, which allows you to change direction. Without wings, you can't turn tightly. - **Slow flight:** Wings allow a plane to stay in the air at much lower speeds. A jet engine alone must be at very high speed to maintain vertical climb. With wings, you can fly slowly and still generate support. ### The Fighter Jet's "Small Wings" Trick You also mentioned the small wings. How can such tiny wings lift such a heavy plane when it's flying horizontally? Answer: **Speed and Angle of Attack.** - **Lift depends partly on speed.** The faster you go, the more air flows over the wings, and the more lift they produce. Fighter jets fly at enormous speeds (300–1500 mph). A small wing at 500 mph can generate the same lift as a huge airliner wing at 150 mph. - **Lift also depends on Angle of Attack (AoA)** – the angle at which the wing meets the air. Fighter pilots can tilt the plane's nose up and "bite" into the air at a steep angle, increasing lift (up to a point). This allows them to turn incredibly tightly using those small wings. When a fighter pilot wants to climb straight up, they pull the nose all the way to 90 degrees. At that point, the wing's angle of attack is effectively zero (it's slicing through the air sideways), so **the wings stop making lift**. The entire job falls on the engine's raw power. ### Summary | Scenario | What Keeps the Plane Up? | How the Wings Help | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | **Normal level flight** | Wings create **lift** (upward force) | The airfoil shape generates lift due to forward speed. | | **Vertical climb** | Engine creates **thrust** (upward force) | Wings are nearly useless; they just create drag. The engine must be stronger than gravity. | | **Tight turn** | Wings create **lift** (angled sideways) | The pilot uses high speed and angle of attack to generate huge forces from small wings. | So, in a vertical climb, a fighter jet is essentially a **rocket with wings attached** – the wings are just along for the ride until the plane levels out again.
